Relevant columns and their potential usage
• GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.PLANET_FEATURES  
  – “feature_type”: restrict to ‘multipolygons’.  
  – “all_tags”: JSON array ‑ flatten to find {key:’boundary’, value:’administrative’}.  
  – “geometry”: WKB geometry of the boundary – cast with TO_GEOGRAPHY() for spatial tests.  
  – “osm_way_id” / “osm_id”: OSM identifier of the boundary polygon (asked in the task).

• GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.PLANET_NODES  
  – “all_tags”: JSON array – flatten to find {key:’amenity’, …}.  
  – “geometry”: WKB for many nodes (else build point from “longitude” / “latitude”).  
  – “longitude”, “latitude”: fallback to create points when “geometry” is NULL.  
  – “id”: node id used for counting POIs that fall inside each boundary.

Typical conditions
• pf.“feature_type” = ‘multipolygons’  
• boundary_tag.value:"key" = 'boundary' AND boundary_tag.value:"value" = 'administrative'  
• poi_tag.value:"key" = 'amenity'     -- to keep points of interest  
• ST_CONTAINS( TO_GEOGRAPHY(pf.“geometry”), node_geom ) = TRUE


-- -----------------------------------------------------------
-- 10 exploratory queries (limit 20 rows each)
-- -----------------------------------------------------------

```sql
-- 1.  Sample multipolygons that are administrative boundaries
SELECT pf."osm_way_id",
       pf."all_tags"
FROM   GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.GEO_OPENSTREETMAP.PLANET_FEATURES pf,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => pf."all_tags") tag
WHERE  pf."feature_type" = 'multipolygons'
  AND  tag.value:"key"  = 'boundary'
  AND  tag.value:"value" = 'administrative'
LIMIT 20;
```

